>
  <p>If your windows look clean from a distance but have white spots or a cloudy haze that regular glass cleaner won't touch, you're looking at hard water stains. They're especially common on the lower windows of homes with in-ground sprinkler systems — a familiar sight in newer Florence, Independence, and Edgewood neighborhoods.</p>

  <h2>What Hard Water Stains Are</h2>

  <p>Tap water carries dissolved calcium and magnesium. When a drop dries on glass, the water evaporates and the minerals stay. One drop leaves a faint ring; a sprinkler hitting the same window every morning for a summer builds a crust.</p>

  <p>There are two stages, and telling them apart matters:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Surface deposits</strong> sit on top of the glass. They feel slightly rough and come off with the right treatment.</li>
    <li><strong>Etching</strong> happens when deposits stay long enough to chemically attack the glass surface. Once the minerals are removed, a faint permanent cloudiness or pitting remains.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="callout">
    <p><strong>Quick test:</strong> Clean a spotted area with a hard-water remover and dry it. If the glass is smooth and clear, it was surface deposits. If it's smooth but still looks hazy or pitted in the light, the glass is etched.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>How to Remove Light Hard Water Spots Yourself</h2>

  <ol>
    <li><strong>Wash the glass first</strong> with soapy water so you're not grinding dirt into it.</li>
    <li><strong>Apply white vinegar or a commercial hard-water remover</strong> and keep the area wet for 5–10 minutes. On vertical glass, a soaked microfiber towel pressed against the spot helps.</li>
    <li><strong>Scrub with a non-scratch pad</strong> (the white kind, not green) while it's still wet.</li>
    <li><strong>Rinse well and squeegee or dry</strong>, then check the result in direct sunlight.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>Avoid green scrub pads, steel wool coarser than 0000, scouring powders, and razor blades on spots you can't identify. Never use these on tinted windows or windows with aftermarket film.</p>

  <h2>How Professionals Remove Heavy Buildup</h2>

  <p>Years-old crust needs more than vinegar. The professional process usually looks like this:</p>

  <ol>
    <li><strong>Test a small area</strong> to gauge how heavy the deposits are and confirm the glass isn't coated or tinted.</li>
    <li><strong>Apply a dedicated mineral remover</strong> — typically a mild acid formulated for glass — and let it dwell to loosen the bond.</li>
    <li><strong>Agitate with 0000 steel wool or a glass-safe pad</strong> while the surface stays wet.</li>
    <li><strong>Polish stubborn haze</strong> with a cerium oxide glass polish if needed. This can also soften the appearance of light etching.</li>
    <li><strong>Rinse thoroughly and protect.</strong> Acid residue left on frames or sills can damage them, so everything gets rinsed. A hydrophobic sealant afterward helps water bead off.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>Heavy stain removal takes several minutes per pane, which is why it's priced as an add-on to a normal window cleaning. See the <a href="/guides/window-cleaning-cost/">cost guide</a> for how it fits into a quote.</p>

  <h2>Stopping Hard Water Stains From Coming Back</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Adjust sprinkler heads</strong> so the spray pattern stops short of the house, or switch beds along the foundation to drip irrigation.</li>
    <li><strong>Check gutters and downspouts</strong> for overflow that sheets down the windows.</li>
    <li><strong>Redirect AC condensate</strong> lines that drip on glass.</li>
    <li><strong>Don't let hose water dry</strong> on windows when washing the car or siding — squeegee it off.</li>
    <li><strong>Seal the glass</strong> with a hydrophobic product after cleaning, and keep up with regular cleanings so spots never get a chance to bond.</li>
  </ul>
